# .trivyignore — Trivy filesystem scan suppressions
#
# Each entry suppresses a path (glob) from secret and config scanning.
# Only suppress paths that have been reviewed and confirmed as either:
#   (a) false positives from third-party library documentation, or
#   (b) runtime-generated artifacts that are gitignored and never committed.

# ── Third-party virtual environments ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The fastapi_users library (bearer.py) embeds a hardcoded example JWT token in
# its OpenAPI schema documentation. The compiled .pyc bytecode therefore triggers
# secret scanners.  These directories contain ONLY third-party packages and are
# already covered by *.pem / .venv / *.pyc rules in .gitignore.
.venv/
apps/backend/.venv/

# ── Runtime-generated TLS certificates ────────────────────────────────────────
# Every ${CB_DATA_DIR}/tls that this repo's own tooling can create inside the
# working tree. They hold self-signed EC certificates generated on first start:
#   openssl req -x509 -nodes -days 365 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1
# by docker/entrypoint-mono.sh for the container paths and by scripts/dev-tls.sh
# for the native ones. All are ephemeral development material, gitignored, and
# never committed.
#
# The list is not left to memory: tests/build/test_secret_suppression_scope.py
# reads the CB_DATA_DIR values out of the Makefile, the shell scripts and the
# compose files, and fails the build if one of them has no entry here. That test
# exists because apps/backend/.dev-data/tls/ had no entry: `make dev` writes a
# self-signed key there, and from that moment on `make verify` failed its own
# security gate on every run, on any machine that had started the dev stack. A
# pre-push gate that goes red on a state the documented workflow produces is a
# gate people learn to bypass.
docker/data/tls/
docker/circuitbreaker-data/tls/
apps/backend/.dev-data/tls/
circuitbreaker-data/tls/

# ── Claude agent worktrees ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .claude/worktrees/ contains agent checkout copies that duplicate source files
# already scanned from their canonical locations.
.claude/worktrees/

# ── Dockerfile.mono root user (DS-0002) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Dockerfile.mono intentionally starts as root: supervisord requires root to
# manage embedded system services (postgres, nats, redis, nginx). The breaker
# user (uid 1000) is created and used for application processes. The entrypoint
# drops privileges after initial volume ownership setup.
# See: checkov:skip=CKV_DOCKER_3,CKV_DS_0002 justification in Dockerfile.mono.
AVD-DS-0002
